WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.904
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.454 --> 00:00:08.370
MARSH GARDINER: Hi, my
name is Marsh Gardiner.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.590
I'm a product
manager for Google.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.750
I tend to work on
Apigee's developer

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.530
portals and Google Cloud
Endpoints' portals as well.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:21.060
I also work on our
open API-based tooling,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.790
and represent Google on the
governing board for the Open

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.890
API Initiative, and chair
the marketing group,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.320
and serve on the Technical
Steering Committee that guides

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.403
the evolution of the spec.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.620
Just a reminder, this is
better API design with OpenAPI.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.779
This is an intro
level discussion.

00:00:39.779 --> 00:00:41.820
We're not going to get
deep into the weeds on how

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.900
the specification works.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.660
But we will talk specs a lot.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.720
In general, we're going
to cover four areas.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.170
One, I'll give you a bit of
a background on OpenAPI Two,

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.360
we'll review specifications
and how they work

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.010
throughout the API lifecycle.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.540
Three, we'll cover some
basic API design principles.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.310
And that will set us up
to combine all of these

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.110
so that we can talk about
how OpenAPI's opinions

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.270
guide, somewhat, API design and
the choices you have to make.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.680
There's a spoiler.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.480
OpenAPI is not going
to magically make

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.190
your API awesome.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.054
But it can help.

00:01:17.054 --> 00:01:18.470
And hopefully, by
the end of this,

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.510
you have a bit of an
understanding about how

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.100
that helps and why it matters.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.360
Who here uses OpenAPI in
their organizations today?

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:31.980
Great.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.985
Who's thinking
about using OpenAPI?

00:01:34.985 --> 00:01:36.930
Good.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.050
Who here still calls it Swagger?

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:39.830
Be honest.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:42.380
Let's see the hands.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.567
Well, it's my marketing
hat on for OpenAPI.

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.150
One of our challenges
is to get people

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.040
to use the name
OpenAPI when referring

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.000
to the specifications.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.020
Swagger still means the tools.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.470
And that is still a
perfectly appropriate way

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.849
to talk about the tooling
that SmartBear oversees.

00:01:57.849 --> 00:01:59.390
But the specification,
it's important

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.090
that we use the right name
because this is an industry

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.190
format, an industry standard.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.480
But behavior change is hard.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.850
The good news is those were
fewer hands than last year

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:10.755
by percentages.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.130
So I think we're
making progress.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.590
So briefly, to set the stage,
before we had this thing

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.000
called Swagger.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.692
The state-of-the-art back
in 2010 was still WADL,

00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:27.400
the Web Application
Description Language,

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.736
and WSDL for SOAP APIs.

00:02:30.736 --> 00:02:34.820
They'd been around awhile, and
they were pretty successful.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.420
But there were also
XML-based formats.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.570
Because of JSON
and JavaScript, we

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.930
began to see the rise of
JSON-based APIs over HTTP.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.970
And it kind of sucks to describe
JSON-based APIs using XML.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:58.370
And in 2010, a couple
of us at Apigee

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.300
took note of a new API
documentation approach

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.070
that a company
called Wordnik was

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.714
pioneering for their own APIs.

00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:07.130
And we thought it
was pretty cool.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.190
So we reached out to them,
which began a conversation

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.080
in an Apigee office
about whether they would

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.010
be interested in Creative
Commons licensing, the look

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.200
and feel of their
API documentation.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.990
And to our surprise,
they said, actually,

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.000
we're thinking of open
sourcing all our tools.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.980
We thought that
sounded pretty great.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.830
And we talked and
laughed about how much

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.980
it sucks to describe
JSON-based APIs with XML.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.440
And I think was
Zeke who said, why

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.450
waddle when you can swagger?

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.550
And we all laughed and
thought that was funny.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.440
And then someone said, actually,
that'd make a great name.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.450
And so it began.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.611
Wordnik had created all of these
tools to serve their own needs.

00:03:46.611 --> 00:03:48.860
And so in the beginning, for
instance, the description

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.500
format that they'd
created, you couldn't

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.510
specify how you would do
authentic authorization.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.530
Obviously, other
people needed that.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.280
And so as the community
began to participate,

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.720
the format began to
become more descriptive.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.530
It was also very much bottom-up.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:13.070
It was one company trying
to solve their own problems

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:13.970
with software.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:17.240
And it wasn't a
vendor saying, I think

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.490
everyone should do it this
way or a consortium saying,

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.039
hey, everybody, like so.

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:22.580
So it had real
authenticity about it.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.140
It was solving real
problems for real people.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.800
And it grew organically
over the next few years.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.330
And then in 2014, that
was about the time

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:34.970
where we were at Apigee,
getting kind of tired of using

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.680
WADL as our preferred format.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.250
And we took a survey to
see how the API description

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:41.990
landscape looked.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.000
We still really liked
what Swagger was doing.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.180
And so we approached Tony
and suggested perhaps

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.770
it was time to consider a
major revision to the spec.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.500
And that kicked off
a working group.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.490
It introduced things
like the YAML format,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.810
at the time, Swagger
specifications.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.700
It did things, like
it added a single file

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.560
expression of a spec.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.380
Before that, there were others,
a bastardized version you

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.297
could do that was embarrassing.

00:05:08.297 --> 00:05:09.630
It was really multi-file format.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.088
And it turned out to be a pain
when you wanted to do things

00:05:12.088 --> 00:05:14.480
like create an editor to
make it easy to create

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.455
a spec in a browser.

00:05:16.455 --> 00:05:17.330
It also added things.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.161
We called them, at the
time, vendor extensions.

00:05:20.161 --> 00:05:22.410
Those would be renamed
specification extensions later.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.872
But really, it's because
we were realizing

00:05:24.872 --> 00:05:26.330
that, as providers,
we needed a way

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.435
to mix in a bunch of
implementation details.

00:05:28.435 --> 00:05:29.810
So that's an
important piece here

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.840
is that the Swagger
specification grew really

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.209
as a way to document the
contract for clients.

00:05:37.209 --> 00:05:39.500
But there was this interesting
tension between the fact

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.510
that while it's
client first, it's

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.610
also really useful
to the server side

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.310
and how providers are
describing their own services

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:47.760
for their own needs in the back.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.810
I'm going to talk about
that a bit more later.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.612
And we began to see
with the 2.0 spec,

00:05:55.612 --> 00:05:57.320
it was like the world
was waiting for it.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.365
And I'll show you that
in a slide in a second.

00:05:59.365 --> 00:06:01.610
It began to take off.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.490
It also became more
important to get it

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.430
into a neutrally-governed
foundation.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.820
And along the way, Wordnik
sold some of its interests

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:17.660
in Swagger to SmartBear and some
its other technologies to NDN.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.250
And it became even
more important

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.840
that if this was going to
be an industry standard,

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.470
it had to have
neutral governance.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.250
And so the Open API
Initiative was founded.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.370
The spec was contributed
by SmartBear.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.257
And all of that
led to figuring out

00:06:30.257 --> 00:06:32.090
the right structure and
organization for how

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.540
we would evolve the spec.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.890
And that all led to
the Open API version 3

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.740
specification, which took
a lot longer than anybody

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.720
had planned.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:42.830
It took us a lot of time.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.490
And that was released
about a year ago.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.619
And since then, we've
seen tooling adopting it.

00:06:46.619 --> 00:06:49.160
And it's starting to get some
pretty good momentum around it.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:52.570
This is why.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:56.965
You can see, in
2014, when we started

00:06:56.965 --> 00:06:58.630
the working group,
that's when interest

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:01.120
began to really take off.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.720
It continued to accelerate as
we added the neutral governance

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:05.972
model around all of this.

00:07:05.972 --> 00:07:07.680
It's like the world
was waiting for this.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.260
And I stopped tracking
these numbers.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.970
This is the Stack Overflow tag
trends for the various formats

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.210
as of 2017.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:20.842
OpenAPI just continued
to accelerate.

00:07:20.842 --> 00:07:23.050
And part of that is because
of this governance model.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.010
So for those of you who
are really interested,

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:26.980
you can go read all about how
the inner workings of this

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:27.400
happen.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:28.120
But it's pretty interesting.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.710
I had never gone through
this process before.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.390
How do you take this
open source project

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:33.490
and make it a safe
space for lots of people

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.060
to participate in?

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.110
And that means doing things like
incorporating, and making sure

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:41.210
that everyone's assets
are properly protected,

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.741
and there's a level
playing field for folks.

00:07:44.741 --> 00:07:45.990
It's pretty interesting stuff.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.780
So we became a project
of the Linux Foundation

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:53.390
and continue to this day
under their auspices.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.181
And we have all of these
guidelines and charters.

00:07:57.181 --> 00:07:58.680
If you're really
excited about this,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.120
you can read this
on OpenAPIs.org.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.970
We grew from an original
founding members of 9.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:08.360
We're now, I think, at 32, with
a pretty impressive collection

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:09.330
of logos.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.560
These are all the members
in the OpenAPI Initiative--

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.590
everything from Big,
Salesforce, Google, IBM,

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.910
Microsoft down to
small players, who

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.700
are focused more on narrow
sections of tooling.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.460
But it's pretty robust,
and it continues to grow.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.549
The reason why all these people
came together to work on this

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.674
is because having a common
industry format to describe

00:08:35.674 --> 00:08:37.590
APIs, it's really important
to a lot of people

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:38.789
and the industry as a whole.

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.970
And this is why I said earlier,
using the specification name

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.270
OpenAPI really does matter.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:47.290
It's important that this thing
be able to live on its own.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:48.850
And I think it's
been doing great.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:49.850
We have more work to do.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.270
The specification itself evolves
and is maintained on GitHub.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.700
So if you want to read
through the specification,

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.010
or participate, or ask
questions, or file issues,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:06.330
this is a great place to go.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:11.000
And all of this is guided by the
Technical Steering Committee.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.970
We started as six,
shrank down to four,

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:17.590
and we're back up to six again.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.420
We meet once a week, in general,
for about an hour and a half.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.220
That's an open
meeting, except when

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:25.430
we have to deal with
internal governance issues.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:27.380
These happen on Thursdays.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:28.700
And they're open to anyone.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.890
You can actually
find the calendar

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.600
through the GitHub repo.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.370
We have all this
documented in how

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:37.610
you can participate in the
development guidelines, et

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:38.450
cetera.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:39.760
That's me.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.829
I thought it was
really cool in 2007

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:44.120
to be trapped inside an iPhone.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.400
And I just haven't
updated my avatar.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:53.900
So the purpose of this body
is really to govern the spec.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.846
It's important to consider
the burden on tooling authors

00:09:57.846 --> 00:09:59.470
when you're making
changes to the spec.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.430
Because any time
you add something,

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:04.070
it's more work for people to
do, or if you make a change

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.720
and now, things
are incompatible.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.879
Really, the way the spec evolves
is by the community needs.

00:10:10.879 --> 00:10:12.461
But someone has to
make sure that it's

00:10:12.461 --> 00:10:13.670
evolving at the right pace.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.540
And that's what we do.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.839
But also, it's work-like,
writing requests

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:21.380
to make changes to
the specification,

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:24.080
clarifying things along the way.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.660
If you get excited about
API description formats,

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.352
you should come
talk to us and join.

00:10:29.352 --> 00:10:31.560
Right now, we're meeting on
Thursdays in the morning.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.400
So we've had the 3.0
spec out for a year.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:41.530
And we spent some of
that time thinking

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.800
about how to better
involve the community,

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.020
how to have clearer
practices and guidelines

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.432
for participation.

00:10:48.432 --> 00:10:50.140
We also have a big
list of possibilities.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.870
And we've spent a lot over of
the last few months talking

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.631
about these things.

00:10:54.631 --> 00:10:56.380
I promised earlier I
wouldn't dig too deep

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.670
into the specification,
but these

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.290
are the kinds of things
that we're thinking about.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.831
And this is really what we need
input from the community for.

00:11:01.831 --> 00:11:05.410
So again, things
like reusable groups.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:08.860
That's the idea that you
could define things and refer

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:12.310
back to them with being able
to write less boilerplate over

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.240
and over.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.130
Things like alternative
schemas, if you

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:16.720
don't want to use
JSON schema, or even

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.469
if you want to use a
newer version of JSON

00:11:18.469 --> 00:11:21.400
schema other than the one
we support officially.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.120
Those are things that
we're considering.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.500
We've talked about
adding multisecond paths.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.350
Actually, I'm super-excited
about overlays.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.060
That's the idea that
you could separate

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.950
some of the
implementation concerns

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.490
from the specification itself.

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:39.562
So rather than mixing in all of
your specification extensions,

00:11:39.562 --> 00:11:41.020
those implementation
level details,

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.811
to be able to have a
separate file that you

00:11:42.811 --> 00:11:45.857
can think of as a layer on top,
you would merge those down.

00:11:45.857 --> 00:11:47.440
Again, I would
encourage anybody who's

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.450
interested in these things
to please participate.

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.533
It's the community involvement
that makes us move.

00:11:55.574 --> 00:11:57.490
These are the various
ways you can contribute.

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:00.410
So I would recommend visiting
the project on GitHub.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:03.100
There are issues you could
file or participate in, pull

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:04.030
requests.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:06.694
We have lots of passionate
arguments about things.

00:12:06.694 --> 00:12:08.860
I would recommend reading
the development guidelines

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.230
that you apply your changes
against the right branch,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.490
that kind of thing.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.172
We have that weekly conference
call that I mentioned.

00:12:15.172 --> 00:12:15.880
We have meet-ups.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.070
And if you want to join
them or even host them,

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:20.760
you should please
get in touch with us.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.280
The easiest way to do that is
via OpenAPI Spec on Twitter.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:29.180
That's probably the easiest
way to quickly reach out to us.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:34.770
But things look pretty good
and please participate.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:39.660
Part 2-- specifications
in the API lifecycle.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.720
So contracts-- OpenAPI is
really a contract-based approach

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:44.650
to things.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:46.870
And really, this is an
agreement between the client

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:47.630
and the server.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.320
So the server says,
hey, this is the way

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:52.210
I want people to talk to me.

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.100
And if you talk to
me, I can promise

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:55.599
that I'm going to
be somewhat stable

00:12:55.599 --> 00:12:56.710
and be around for awhile.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:59.260
And the client's like,
hey, that's cool.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.150
And so you register
an application.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.250
And you are able to make
requests against that.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.670
But I really do think of
it as this formal contract

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:08.440
and agreement between
these two sides.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:14.157
I'm going to ask you to
use your imagination.

00:13:14.157 --> 00:13:16.490
This is, as you can tell, in
the style of Homer Simpson.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:19.270
But Legal would not let me use
Homer Simpson in my slides.

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:22.280
So mentally substitute
this with Homer Simpson,

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.440
because apparently that's OK.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.030
Sweet, sweet declarative,
compile-time contracts.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.990
I actually imagine
myself as Homer Simpson

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:31.900
when I think about
OpenAPI in this way.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.050
What it is, is I love that
you're so declarative,

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:39.020
you can understand all the
various capabilities of an API.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:40.460
It's very declarative.

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:42.830
And what I mean
by compile time is

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:45.800
that, as you build
your application,

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:48.330
the interface is hardened.

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:51.410
You compare this to things
like HATEOAS, Hypertext

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.120
As The Engine Of
Application State,

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.980
where the client
can only do what

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.190
the server says is allowable
from the state that you're in.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.050
This is much more declarative.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:01.894
And you can think
of it as slices

00:14:01.894 --> 00:14:04.310
of functionality that you can
expect will work in this way

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:05.450
consistently.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:16.050
A contract defines the
boundary of responsibility.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.570
And they're great because
this formal agreement lets

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:22.200
the client and server teams
understand the expected

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.510
behaviors so that I
know what to expect back

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:25.620
when I ask in this way.

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.685
Contracts have been
around for a long time.

00:14:30.685 --> 00:14:34.490
I've already mentioned
WSDL and WADL.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:38.460
Before that, we had
things like CORBA.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.234
And we've had other
contracts as well.

00:14:40.234 --> 00:14:41.400
There are different flavors.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:44.040
I'm going to mention
HATEOAS before GraphQL.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.320
But what I like about
OpenAPI, as I mentioned,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.620
is its declarative nature.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.770
And as I mentioned
earlier, it really

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:54.689
grew out of putting
the client first.

00:14:54.689 --> 00:14:56.730
What is it that people
are going to know in order

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:57.930
to be able to call my API?

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.830
Google doesn't use
OpenAPI for its own APIs.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:07.170
Why?

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:10.660
Well, Google runs things
at really big scale.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:14.520
And Google needs to have
a binary-based format.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.930
And so they use proto buffers.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:19.464
And they have open sourced
something called GRPC.

00:15:19.464 --> 00:15:20.880
And if you come
in within a couple

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.600
of orders of magnitude
of Google scale,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:24.460
you might consider it too.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.630
But we also recognize
that exposing our APIs

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:28.944
with JSON flavors is important.

00:15:28.944 --> 00:15:30.610
So we have a gateway
that sits in front.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.980
And it basically takes
that GRPC and exposes it

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:36.520
as JSON over HTTP.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.310
Most of our APIs can be
expressed as open API

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:40.870
in that way.

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:43.930
The way I think about this is
that if you like contracts,

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:47.830
and you've got scale
problems, GRPC is a great fit.

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:50.893
If you have adoption problems,
I would go with OpenAPI.

00:15:54.454 --> 00:15:56.620
If any of you have attended
any of Apigee's sessions

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:58.810
before, you've probably
seen a slide like this that

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:01.750
is how we think about the
API lifecycle in general,

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:05.600
the core lifecycle being
those icons in the center.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.780
And this covers a lot.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:10.460
And if you ever look at Kin
Lane, the API Evangelist site,

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:12.320
he's got pretty similar lists.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.690
API management's has
been around for awhile.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:16.112
These categories
are pretty good.

00:16:16.112 --> 00:16:17.570
It looks like people
want pictures.

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:18.470
Wait one second.

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:20.130
3, 2, 1.

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:21.890
Good.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:26.022
I want to focus on just
the core lifecycle bits.

00:16:26.022 --> 00:16:27.980
This is everything from
imagining and designing

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.050
your APIs through
deploying, publishing,

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:33.470
and maintaining those,
analyzing that traffic

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.460
and how you then
maintain it over time,

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.760
protect against threats,
real-time monitoring.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.570
But what's interesting to me
is how many of these stages

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.210
are better informed
by specifications.

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:46.670
They add value in lots
of different stages

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.271
of the lifecycle.

00:16:48.271 --> 00:16:50.270
And I'll walk you through
a couple of these just

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:51.830
to give you a flavor
of what that is.

00:16:54.577 --> 00:16:56.160
Documentation is the
most obvious one.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:00.240
For many years, people
thought Swagger was Swagger UI

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:02.594
just because they'd
see the documentation

00:17:02.594 --> 00:17:03.510
and think that was it.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.000
It was really a much
deeper and layered system.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.490
But specs-driving documentation
is the number one reason

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.280
that gets people to
write specifications,

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.810
in my experience.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:16.004
Everyone understands that
in order for a developer

00:17:16.004 --> 00:17:18.420
to consume your API, they have
to understand how it works.

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:19.419
They need documentation.

00:17:19.419 --> 00:17:21.480
Maintaining
documentation is a pain.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.109
And doing that by hand
is kind of even worse.

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:29.970
And whether you treat
your API specifications

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:34.200
as truth or possible truth--
and what I mean by that is,

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.020
say you're generating your API
specifications from annotations

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:38.320
in your source code.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:40.980
You can consider that to
be truth, more or less.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:43.980
If you write that description
and maintain it out-of-band,

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.410
it is a description
of a possible truth.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:49.670
It may be just as true as the
ones driven from annotations.

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:54.630
But in the case where you're
writing it out-of-band,

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:57.360
that's where you may want to
do automated tests to make sure

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.600
that your specification
stays, in fact, in line

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.850
with what you're actually
exposing with your APIs.

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:08.580
So specs-driving documentation
is the single biggest reason

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:12.490
to use an OpenAPI spec today.

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.110
But there are lots of others.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.060
So we use specs.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:16.650
This is in Apigee Edge.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.940
When you go to create a proxy,
which you do, essentially,

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.340
in order to be able to
attach policies to control

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:26.100
as a part of API management,
how you control traffic

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:29.460
and access to the services
that you're offering,

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:34.480
if you have described
your API using OpenAPI,

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:36.910
we can use that information
to help you figure out

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:39.910
how to manage your API.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.860
Specs also can inform
implementations

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:45.640
you may generate code in the
back end, whether it's stubs,

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:50.030
or whether it's something
more sophisticated.

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:51.670
Specs can drive mocks.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:54.830
This one seems
obvious in many ways.

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:57.020
But it's surprising
how few organizations

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:58.684
take advantage of it.

00:18:58.684 --> 00:19:00.100
The nice thing
about driving mocks

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.080
is it lets you start building
your client in parallel

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.202
with the server implementation.

00:19:05.202 --> 00:19:06.910
That's a pretty good
benefit right there.

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.951
Now, you don't have to
wait for the server to get

00:19:08.951 --> 00:19:13.680
stood up before you can
begin to build your client.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.800
It also is a good way to
just put it through its paces

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.030
as you're in the design phase.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:23.130
Or say you're a financial
services company,

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.680
and you don't want
your developers

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.190
making live requests to your
actual production backends.

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:31.470
You could attach that to a mock.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.991
And have them send requests
without using real money.

00:19:33.991 --> 00:19:35.490
More sophisticated,
of course, would

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:37.890
be to have a parallel system
that didn't use real money.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:41.930
But this is a good way to start.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.310
Specs can also power
test generation.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:48.750
Here is a snapshot of a project
from a colleague of mine,

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:49.441
Noah Deitz.

00:19:49.441 --> 00:19:50.940
He has a project
called OATTs, which

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:53.359
is OpenAPI Test Templates.

00:19:53.359 --> 00:19:55.650
These are ways in which you
can make assertions and run

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:58.231
automated tests that
help keep your API

00:19:58.231 --> 00:20:00.480
specification in line with
what you're actually doing.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:01.188
It's pretty neat.

00:20:01.188 --> 00:20:03.240
I encourage you to check it out.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:07.470
You could use specifications
to classify traffic at runtime

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:10.620
or to validate
payloads at runtime.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.500
I mentioned earlier
generating SDKs

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.980
for clients or to
do server side code.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:21.040
There's a lot that specs do.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.650
But given that this
talk is about API design

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:28.215
and how that OpenAPI
influences it and its opinions,

00:20:28.215 --> 00:20:29.590
I want to really
talk about this.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:33.000
This is the spec
editor in Apigee Edge.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.500
But you've probably seen lots
of these out in the wild.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.900
As you incorporate
specifications

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.825
early in your process,
you get better APIs.

00:20:45.825 --> 00:20:47.450
I'm going to talk
about this a lot more

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:49.710
for the rest of the talk.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:55.260
But being able to design
and see your API--

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:59.790
when I go back to 2014
when we started the working

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:03.150
group for Swagger 2,
people didn't actually

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.550
understand at the time why
having a YAML-based syntax

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:06.050
mattered.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.100
And it matters, so we
can do things like this.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.510
The ability to write
in a web browser

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:14.640
a specification,
that's a big deal.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.650
And the ability to get
real-time documentation,

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:18.024
this is like
documentation-driven

00:21:18.024 --> 00:21:18.930
development.

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:22.074
And it's a way in which you can
make better APIs before you've

00:21:22.074 --> 00:21:23.240
even written a line of code.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:31.980
So before we go into the
OpenAPI influence on API design,

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.830
I want to review some
key API design principles

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:39.665
that we will then apply to
that and bring it all together.

00:21:39.665 --> 00:21:41.930
Did anybody read this book?

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:43.040
It's been out for awhile.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:43.790
I see a few hands.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:44.330
Good.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:45.900
I would encourage you to.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:48.920
You can find this on
our website, Apigee.com.

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:50.870
Even though it was
published in 2012,

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:52.730
it continues to be
really relevant.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:54.245
It's been cited
by organizations,

00:21:54.245 --> 00:21:58.410
like whitehouse.gov
in the Obama years.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.360
It continues to influence
a lot of thinking.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:02.360
And it's a pretty simple
pragmatic approach

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:05.365
to building interfaces
that developers love.

00:22:05.365 --> 00:22:07.490
And a number of the examples
that I'm going to pull

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:08.390
come from this.

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:10.842
And this was really gathered
from collecting the patterns

00:22:10.842 --> 00:22:13.300
that we were seeing that we
were working with our customers

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.179
and working out in the wild and
then synthesizing that down.

00:22:17.179 --> 00:22:19.220
So it's an evidence-based
approach to API design.

00:22:22.146 --> 00:22:23.520
There are four
patterns from this

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:24.770
that I want to call out today.

00:22:24.770 --> 00:22:26.760
If you want more,
please read the book.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:31.480
But one, reuse, don't reinvent.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:31.980
Obvious.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:35.145
Again, these are all
basically common sense.

00:22:35.145 --> 00:22:38.550
That's all design is really is
common sense and creativity.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:40.480
Two, resource-oriented.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:43.090
And when I say that,
I mean the R in URL.

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:45.510
Three, I'm going to
talk about names and why

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:48.510
they are so critically
important in this area.

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:51.570
Then four, just a general
principle-- when in doubt,

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.640
do what's best for developers.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.525
And by that, I mean
your consumers.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.030
Reuse, don't reinvent.

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.610
Web technologies-- when we
talk about web API design,

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.180
for me, the first web API that
I saw and fell in love with

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:13.290
was Twitter sometime
in 2007, I would say.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.540
And what was amazing
to me about Twitter

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:18.480
was that I understood
how to use their web

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.540
service, the actual
website, and use Twitter

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.090
as a user of their
web interface.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:28.416
But switching from that to
their API was really easy.

00:23:28.416 --> 00:23:30.540
Back then, you could use
your basic authentication.

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:33.081
The same credentials you were
using to sign into to the site,

00:23:33.081 --> 00:23:35.100
you would use to
sign your requests.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.170
And the way they'd
organize their API

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.670
was very similar
in its hierarchy

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.295
to how their website
had been designed.

00:23:40.295 --> 00:23:41.939
I mean, that's not an accident.

00:23:41.939 --> 00:23:43.980
And so it was just so
amazing and beautiful to me

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:46.530
that I could get at
my data, not just

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.660
in the way that they said
I could, but in the way

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.770
that I wanted to.

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:55.700
And a lot of what made the web
work is what makes web APIs

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:57.470
work too.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:01.130
So JavaScript is the
lingua Franca of the web.

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:07.340
It is why JSON emerged as a
rival and successor to XML.

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:10.480
So JSON is a key
piece of web APIs.

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:14.450
And HTTP is another great thing.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:18.780
If you think back to right
before this style of JSON

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:23.527
over HTP came out, everyone
was doing RPC, more or less.

00:24:23.527 --> 00:24:24.610
And that's perfectly fine.

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:28.190
It just wasn't leveraging what
was really great about the web.

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:29.690
It was tunneling
a bunch of things,

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:33.080
like the actions
through the payload.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:36.660
And by using what makes HTP and
the web great, we got web APIs.

00:24:39.796 --> 00:24:41.420
And that's important
enough to call out

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:42.990
they are resource-oriented.

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:45.350
So if you use the
web, you understand

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:46.980
that things have locations.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:48.860
And this is why it
was so easy for me

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:53.199
when I first got into APIs
and web APIs through Twitter,

00:24:53.199 --> 00:24:55.490
it was because I already
understood how the web worked.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:57.740
I knew that oh, you go to a
blog, and there are posts,

00:24:57.740 --> 00:24:59.470
and then posts are entities.

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:02.470
This kind of hierarchy is
reflected in good URL design.

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:05.441
And it just makes it easy to
think about how the site works

00:25:05.441 --> 00:25:07.190
and how to take the
context of the service

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:10.680
and apply it to how
you access its data.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:11.920
It's tremendously powerful.

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:14.580
You don't have to teach the
basic mechanisms of accessing

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:16.040
data and functionality.

00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:21.160
This is third actually--

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:21.820
naming.

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:24.580
So this is one of my favorite
quotes in computer science

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:25.735
by Phil Karlton.

00:25:25.735 --> 00:25:28.220
"There are only two hard
things in computer science--

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.310
cache invalidation
and naming things."

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:36.550
There's a version of this
that wasn't from Phil.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:38.797
Apologies to Phil.

00:25:38.797 --> 00:25:41.130
"There are two hard problems
in computer science-- cache

00:25:41.130 --> 00:25:43.330
validation, naming things,
and off-by-one errors."

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:49.900
But naming-- think how hard
it is to create a good user

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:52.030
interface that's a UI.

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:54.689
It takes a lot of
design and rework

00:25:54.689 --> 00:25:56.230
even before you
write a line of code.

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:59.482
APIs are no different than that.

00:25:59.482 --> 00:26:01.440
So all you have is URL
safe characters, really,

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:05.770
to deal with when you're working
with your resource design.

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:09.400
This means that the names of
things have really great power.

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.120
And one of the things I
like to do in API design

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:16.050
is to involve my tech
writers because you really

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:18.490
want people who actually care
and think about words a lot.

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:21.295
And so not only do
tech writers spend

00:26:21.295 --> 00:26:22.920
a lot of time trying
to think about how

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:26.100
to break complicated things down
to make them easy for people

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.020
who are approaching
a subject area,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:33.300
but they also tend to know a lot
about other parts of your API.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:36.150
And so they can help you
keep your API consistent.

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:38.970
And the third bit, this is what
I just alluded to a minute ago,

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:42.090
is that they care about words.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:44.801
Think about how hard it is
to name a variable well.

00:26:44.801 --> 00:26:45.300
That's hard.

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:51.200
Anybody here play
Adventureland or Zork?

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:52.680
I'm dating myself here.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.540
Oh, no.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:55.040
Wow.

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:58.710
Anyway, I don't have time
to do a demo of that.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.680
But if you're familiar with
it, you were in a room.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.230
You see a lamp on a table,
and you say, get lamp.

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:09.650
And to me, when I'm designing
an API, I'm thinking about that.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:12.780
How do I make it
obvious from these words

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:14.070
how you work with this system?

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.380
The way I think about it
is that your developers

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:21.630
are taking a journey
through your API that's

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:23.730
not unlike Zork.

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.100
And all you have to
work with is text.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:31.010
And it's your job to keep them
from being eaten by a Grue.

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:33.392
It'd be a funnier joke
if you'd all played.

00:27:33.392 --> 00:27:34.790
God!

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:38.540
Anyway, so you have one job.

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:40.700
The API's job is to
make the developer as

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:42.470
successful as possible.

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:45.650
The orientation for APIs is
to think about design choices

00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:48.850
from the application
developer's point-of-view.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:50.600
If you look in the
lower right-hand corner

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:51.975
of this little
blue section, this

00:27:51.975 --> 00:27:56.540
is the Japanese
expression of Shoshin.

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:58.370
My apologies if I'm
saying it poorly.

00:27:58.370 --> 00:27:59.580
I don't speak Japanese.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:01.910
But Shoshin, it's the
concept of beginner's mind.

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:04.610
It's a Zen concept.

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:06.620
So the example I like
to give here is--

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:10.587
who knows what IMEI stands for?

00:28:10.587 --> 00:28:12.920
Wow, I expected more telco
people in the audience today.

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:15.290
It's the International
Mobile Equipment Identity.

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:16.831
If you look in your
phone's settings,

00:28:16.831 --> 00:28:19.190
you'll probably see
your IMEI number.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:22.280
When I saw my first telco-based
API, I thought, what's that?

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:25.400
It's a device ID.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.350
It's that curse of
knowledge that you have

00:28:27.350 --> 00:28:29.930
and that's really hard
to step outside of.

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.000
And that's why taking API
design to other people

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.340
is really important,
specifically

00:28:34.340 --> 00:28:35.900
people who are
possible developers

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.390
outside your organization
and understand

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.130
how they see the
words that you've

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:45.100
chosen because it is difficult
to avoid the bad ones.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:51.750
So Shoshin, the beginner's
mind is another place

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:54.570
I'd like to go when
designing APIs.

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:57.660
So now that we've touched
on specifications,

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:02.400
why they matter, why OpenAPI
is important, and basic design

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:05.510
principles, we can
put them all together.

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:07.790
But as I started
with, just remember,

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:09.500
OpenAPI is not going
to magically make

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.970
your API awesome.

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.310
But it does have some opinions.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:16.280
And I find it's a whole lot
easier to work with them

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:17.420
than to fight them.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:20.134
And this matters whether you
are building micro services,

00:29:20.134 --> 00:29:22.550
or whether you're building
full-blown public API programs.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.270
So in the rest of this
talk, we'll cover--

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:29.228
and I'll make sure there's
time for questions--

00:29:29.228 --> 00:29:33.840
we'll cover these six ways
that OpenAPI guides API design.

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:35.170
One, JSON.

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:36.374
We'll use JSON schema.

00:29:36.374 --> 00:29:38.040
You'll see why that
matters in a second.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:40.290
I'm going to walk you through
each of these in detail.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.890
Two, signatures-- how
the signature mechanism

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:46.820
works in OpenAPI and
why that's important.

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:50.240
Three, warning, do not
vary your response models

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.550
from parameters.

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:54.770
Four, collections
and entities are

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:56.250
a nice pattern and a good fit.

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:57.830
That's from the
API design e-book

00:29:57.830 --> 00:30:00.060
that I mentioned earlier.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:03.200
Five, versioning and how you
can approach it with OpenAPI.

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:05.465
And six, defaults
and pagination.

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:14.700
So OpenAPI leverages
JSON schema.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.519
And it was in a draft at the
time that we launched v3.

00:30:18.519 --> 00:30:20.060
And in fact, we were
a little worried

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:24.930
that it was a dead end
of a specification.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:28.700
But it's been revived
a lot recently.

00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:30.880
But we picked a draft, and
we can't just update it

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:31.880
without breaking things.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.670
That's why when I talked about
the big list of possibilities,

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:36.510
it's on there.

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:40.340
But we didn't take JSON
schema entirely as is.

00:30:40.340 --> 00:30:42.290
One difference between
version 2 and version 3

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:48.460
is that we added any
of and one of to that.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.930
That had been a common request.

00:30:50.930 --> 00:30:52.670
But what's important
to remember,

00:30:52.670 --> 00:30:55.100
and one of the challenges you
face in working with OpenAPI

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:58.760
is that, really, JSON schema is
a validation language and not

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:00.630
a modeling language.

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:06.530
And if you remember that, it
will help you on your journey.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:10.230
Two, anyone recognize this?

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:12.090
When I think of
a signature, it's

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:14.650
John Hancock's, the one
that springs to mind,

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:16.390
that big one in the center.

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:20.812
But if you look really closely,
you can see path and verb.

00:31:20.812 --> 00:31:22.770
A lot of people don't
realize that OpenAPI also

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:23.800
signed the Declaration of
Independence. (WHISPERS)

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:24.383
It's not true.

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:35.110
(NORMAL VOICE) It is a unique
pairing of a path and a verb

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:37.780
that really defines the core
of what's in an OpenAPI spec.

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:39.670
That is its signature.

00:31:39.670 --> 00:31:42.370
And what I mean by that
is that if I'm a gateway,

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:44.140
and there's traffic
passing through,

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:46.780
then I should be
able to identify,

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:51.340
based on what I know from the
specification, which operation

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:53.650
it maps onto in that spec.

00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:55.840
That's the signature mechanism.

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:57.820
I kind of cheated
the plus media type.

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:00.614
I didn't let them sign.

00:32:00.614 --> 00:32:02.530
If you are going to have
multiple media types,

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:04.270
you can use that as a way--

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.430
it's next layer of signature.

00:32:06.430 --> 00:32:08.890
But path and verb--

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:12.280
if you think in terms of
path and verb, it will help.

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:21.490
Number two, don't vary
response models by parameter.

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:23.010
I've tried to protect the--

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:24.570
I shouldn't say guilty.

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:29.649
This API came about,
I think, around 2006.

00:32:29.649 --> 00:32:30.940
So it predates a lot of things.

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:33.700
But you can see that
what we have here is--

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:34.200
where is it?

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:34.727
There we go.

00:32:34.727 --> 00:32:35.560
Oh, I put it in red.

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:36.520
Good.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.890
The method equals.

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:43.210
You are passing in
what the signature is

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:44.740
in the query string.

00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:47.140
And because that can appear
anywhere in the query string,

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:50.340
it can be a lot
harder to figure out

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:52.366
what the signature
mechanism is if you're

00:32:52.366 --> 00:32:53.740
in a layered system
like a proxy.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:33:00.832
When Wordnik set out to build
their own stack for APIs,

00:33:00.832 --> 00:33:03.040
they wanted to have a nice
clean signature mechanism.

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:04.870
And that's why we have
the legacy that we

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:06.596
have today of path and verb.

00:33:06.596 --> 00:33:08.470
And that's a perfectly
good way to do things.

00:33:08.470 --> 00:33:10.690
That's basically the
way the web works.

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:13.791
So just a warning, don't vary
response models by parameters.

00:33:13.791 --> 00:33:14.290
It's funny.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:16.873
This is one of the things that
comes up a lot in the community

00:33:16.873 --> 00:33:20.957
requests for how the next
edition of the spec should go.

00:33:20.957 --> 00:33:22.540
And we continue to
resist it, in part,

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:26.092
because it adds another
burden on tooling authors,

00:33:26.092 --> 00:33:26.800
as I said before.

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:33.080
Three, I think we're up to--

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:35.170
collections and entities.

00:33:35.170 --> 00:33:37.520
And so what I mean by
this is, like, /dogs,

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:39.860
or this could be /posts.

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:41.450
That is the collection.

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:43.550
And then you've got the
specific entity there.

00:33:43.550 --> 00:33:47.090
So if I've got /dogs,
and I do a post to /dogs,

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:48.560
I'm creating a new dog entity.

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:50.620
And I will get back a
unique ID that defines that

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:52.820
and that specific dog entity.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:54.870
And then I can do
things like update it,

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:57.290
or delete it, or get it.

00:33:57.290 --> 00:34:00.590
And so collections and
entities are a really nice way

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:03.665
to represent the
hierarchy of your system.

00:34:03.665 --> 00:34:06.290
And it's a way in which you can
use the names of those entities

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:09.230
to help people understand
how to work with your APIs.

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:11.810
It's also useful
for API management.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:14.659
Because now, because I've
got the signature mechanism

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:18.380
and because I've got this
way in which I can think

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:20.179
about the structure
hierarchically,

00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:22.460
I can then apply policies
to specific areas.

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:24.780
Say I want to throttle--

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.380
maybe throttle and dogs is the
wrong word to put together--

00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:31.310
say I want to throttle
dogs but not cats.

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:34.130
I could decide to do that
because I have these kinds of--

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:37.400
I think I need a better
example than that next time.

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:40.219
Sorry, PETA.

00:34:40.219 --> 00:34:41.860
Another useful thing
that I probably

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:45.260
should have mentioned
during the naming bit

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:48.500
is the singleton resource.

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:49.610
Imagine this was users.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:52.010
And users 1, 2, 3,
4 happened to be me.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:55.520
Having a /me-- and I
forget who pioneered that.

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:57.560
Facebook was early
on that pattern.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:00.110
But it's nice to have these
convenience singleton methods

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:01.550
that map onto these things.

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:04.112
And you can still apply policies
in the same way to that.

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:10.190
Versioning-- nothing causes
more controversy in API design

00:35:10.190 --> 00:35:11.102
than versioning.

00:35:11.102 --> 00:35:12.560
And I'm here to
give you permission

00:35:12.560 --> 00:35:14.940
to do versioning the way
you think is right for you.

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:20.060
However, do think what's
best for your developers.

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:25.720
I remember when Facebook used--
hopefully, they don't do this

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:29.246
anymore-- but it used to be
able to pass in the version you

00:35:29.246 --> 00:35:30.370
wanted in the query string.

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:31.870
And people always
forgot to do that.

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:33.780
And things would break.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:36.310
The downside of
versioning in the resource

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:40.370
is that it feels kind of
wrong to version the resource.

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:41.290
But it's also obvious.

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:42.430
And so you have to
figure out what's

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:44.140
right for your developers
and make that trade-off.

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:46.330
But what I wanted to talk
about is the way in which

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:47.980
OpenAPI has opinions here.

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:50.080
So one of the things we
added in the version 3

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:53.170
of the specification is the
servers replace the host.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:56.600
And we added a way in which
to templatize that server.

00:35:56.600 --> 00:36:00.890
So you can, in fact, in
your server's information,

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:02.450
you can put the version.

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:04.250
Used to be able to do
that with a base URL.

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:06.530
Now, you can do it in
the servers as well.

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:09.440
The only caveat I would give
here and something to be

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:11.780
careful of is that
it may be that you

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:13.130
want a separate specification.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:16.020
If you're putting
versions in your servers,

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.800
you might as well think of
those as different contracts

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:21.650
because someday, you're
going to retire one of them.

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:25.860
But if you choose to
do the Twilio style,

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:28.106
if I remember correctly,
Twilio has got at least 60--

00:36:28.106 --> 00:36:29.480
it's probably got,
like, 100 now.

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:32.570
But they have a lot of
versions that they keep.

00:36:32.570 --> 00:36:34.790
And that works for them.

00:36:34.790 --> 00:36:37.670
And they use a date-based
format to do that.

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:40.074
So I give you permission
to use what works for you.

00:36:40.074 --> 00:36:41.990
But do look at what's
worked for other people.

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:48.070
Defaults and
pagination-- again, I

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:50.020
think this comes back to naming.

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:52.090
Make good choices in
how you name things.

00:36:52.090 --> 00:36:54.622
We took a look around and
saw a bunch of patterns.

00:36:54.622 --> 00:36:56.830
I think my least favorite
was Twitter's, at the time.

00:36:56.830 --> 00:36:59.980
It was RPP, which stood
for Request Per Page.

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:01.960
And that's just not
intuitive, right?

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:05.249
Limit and offset are
nice because people

00:37:05.249 --> 00:37:06.540
who use databases are familiar.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.620
This is common language.

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:11.620
But with OpenAPI, at
least, what I really want

00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:13.570
is a consistent way
to apply pagination

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:15.550
to all of my collections.

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:18.340
And the ability to
define that in one spot

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:20.910
and reuse that definition
over and over again

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:21.910
is really important.

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:23.290
The pagination is
actually a great place

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.206
to use hypermedia too.

00:37:24.206 --> 00:37:27.150
I think it's the most
obvious case for hypermedia.

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:28.990
If you can give
people back a link

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:32.987
to the next page of results,
consider doing that.

00:37:32.987 --> 00:37:35.070
And you should be able to
do those kinds of things

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:37.546
using OpenAPI.

00:37:37.546 --> 00:37:38.920
So here are your
three takeaways,

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:41.280
then I'm going to open
this up to some questions.

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:45.912
One, investing in API design,
it always pays benefits.

00:37:45.912 --> 00:37:47.370
Its so much easier
to make a change

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:48.780
early in that process
than to do it later.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:51.480
We all know this, and yet, we
continue to push it off and not

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:52.870
do it properly.

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:56.430
Invest in API design,
even using specifications

00:37:56.430 --> 00:37:57.750
to help that process go better.

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:04.140
Two, the promise of specs--

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.310
by having a formal
machine-friendly description

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:09.140
of your services, you will
do less work over time.

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:11.350
Levering specifications
throughout the lifecycle

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:12.790
will help you do less work.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:14.230
Yay.

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:18.266
And the third bit is don't
fight OpenAPI's opinions,

00:38:18.266 --> 00:38:19.890
if you're going down
the OpenAPI route.

00:38:19.890 --> 00:38:21.639
Hopefully, you have a
better understanding

00:38:21.639 --> 00:38:23.740
of some of the ways in
which it sees the world.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:25.281
I'm going to add a
number four, which

00:38:25.281 --> 00:38:26.400
is please call it OpenAPI.

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:27.733
It's important for the standard.

00:38:27.733 --> 00:38:30.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

